{"plan_markdown":"# Implementation Plan: CL2 — WBC-backed ledger persistence, import, and replay\n\n## Overview\n\nCL2 lands the WBC-backed critique ledger: it ports the CL1 domain schema and pure replay engine from the paused-epic archive, additively versions them per the 2026-07-28 safety amendment, persists immutable occurrence/reconciliation/disposition/response/outcome/audit events through the existing `SqliteAttemptLedgerStore`/`SqliteLedgerOutbox` substrate, builds three rebuildable read-only projections (cumulative/domain, active-action, per-round accountability), adds a one-time legacy importer, and wires region-freshness vectors and governed tombstone handling. It ends with a written, reviewed handoff to CL3.\n\n**Repository shape (verified):**\n- The current workspace has **no** `arnold/critique_ledger/` directory. The three CL1 modules (`schemas.py` 731 lines, `semantic_loop.py` 1206 lines, `__init__.py`) exist only at `/workspace/.paused-epics/critique-ledger-m9-glm-20260723/Arnold/arnold/critique_ledger/`. That same archive holds the five **cancelled** pre-amendment CL2 artifacts (`cl2_migrations.py`, `m6_importer.py`, `ledger_writer.py`, `wbc_bridge.py`, `manifest_store.py`) which must be audited, never implicitly adopted.\n- The landed WBC substrate in `arnold/workflow/` is exactly what we reuse: `SqliteAttemptLedgerStore` (WAL, `isolation_level=None`, `_append_tx` with BEGIN IMMEDIATE + idempotency-key dedup + exactly-one-terminal + post-terminal-rejection + monotonic sequence + `_begin_immediate_retry`), `SqliteLedgerOutbox.append_event_with_outbox` (atomic event+outbox in one tx), `SqliteLedgerMigrator` (versioned, checksum-validated, forward-fix-only, `Migration(version,name,statements,checksum)` registry, `default_m6a_migrations` pattern of idempotent `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` / `ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN`). `payload_policy.py`/`durable_refs.py` own inline/reference, retention, redaction, ordering, and effect rules — reused, not reimplemented.\n- CL1 `semantic_loop.py` is a **pure** module exposing the replay surface we wrap: `validate_occurrence_custody`, `apply_reconciliation_events`, `apply_disposition_events`, `construct_manifest`, `build_briefing`, `project_reviser_input` (the active-action ancestor), `project_gate_input`, `replay_full`.\n- Tests live under `tests/arnold/...`; substrate wiring convention is `store = SqliteAttemptLedgerStore(path); outbox = SqliteLedgerOutbox(store)`.\n\n**Module layout we create** (all under `arnold/critique_ledger/`, mirroring the CL1 module boundary so the audit boundary against the cancelled archive stays clean — the archive's `cl2_migrations.py` etc. are *different* pre-amendment files):\n- `schemas.py` — ported + additively versioned (cl.schema.v1 + cl.schema.v2)\n- `semantic_loop.py` — ported pure replay (no persistence)\n- `__init__.py` — ported, updated exports\n- `cl2_migrations.py` — NEW: domain-table/index `Migration` registry fed to `SqliteLedgerMigrator`\n- `store.py` — NEW: `CritiqueLedgerStore` domain append/replay-scan layer (wraps `SqliteAttemptLedgerStore` + `SqliteLedgerOutbox`, mirrors `_append_tx`)\n- `projections.py` — NEW: cumulative + active-action + round-accountability projection builders over read-only replay\n- `legacy_import.py` — NEW: one-time importer\n- `freshness.py` — NEW: region-freshness vectors + deterministic tripwires + governed tombstone/unavailable handling\n\n**Constraints that shape every step:** additive schema only (never DROP); occurrences never merged; reconciliation decisions are immutable, correctable evidence; semantic identity assigned only by reconciliation; projections carry no positive authority; replay issues no model call or external effect; start persistence precedes dispatch; terminal failure is `persistence_failed|indeterminate`, never clean success; historical gaps stay explicit; WBC rules reused not reimplemented; no new DB service / event bus / authority plane / dual-write.\n\n## Anchor tension (must surface, not optimize around)\n\nThe idea constraint is **\"Consume only an accepted CL1 handoff\"**, but the only available CL1 handoff has `accepted_for_cl2: false` with **five procedural blockers** (review sign-off, M6 prerequisite incoherence, proof-index failure, ownership decision record, portfolio-gate human approval). All CL1 *technical* must-gates pass (custody, freshness, replay equality, authority, schema, prerequisite checks). The 2026-07-28 safety amendment also states \"CL2 must be replanned from the amended, reviewed handoff before execution resumes,\" yet that amendment is itself an un-reviewed CL1 contract amendment.\n\nThe user direction for this unattended run resolves this explicitly: *\"Planner/executor must make conservative repo-backed assumptions, encode them in artifacts, and let normal critique/gate/review phases reject unsafe choices.\"*\n\n**Decision recorded for this plan (a bridge, not a silent override):** CL2 implementation proceeds against the **archived CL1 schema/replay contracts** — which are technically coherent and repo-verifiable — while the five procedural blockers resolve in parallel. This is a **bridge**, not a contradiction: every artifact encodes the assumption explicitly, the ported code is verifiable against the frozen archive, and CL2 produces no live cutover, no mutation of plan/gate/lifecycle state, and no production backfill (all within CL2 anti-scope). The normal critique/gate/review phases remain the authority that can reject any choice that exceeds the amended contract. **This tension is flagged, not resolved here.** If the portfolio/ownership gates reject proceeding on an unaccepted handoff, this plan blocks at the gate rather than widening schema or ownership — which is the intended fail-safe.\n\n## Questions\n\n1. **[blocking] Can CL2 proceed on the unaccepted CL1 handoff?** Assumption: yes, as a bridge, against the archived schema contracts only, with all assumptions encoded and critique/gate as the rejection authority. Flagged as anchor tension above. *(Resolved by user direction for this run; remains a gate risk.)*\n2. **[assume_and_proceed] Partial-parse admission contract** — not fully defined. Assumption: partially parseable attempts are admitted with a `PARTIAL_RECOVERED` `ParseStatus`, an `evidence_ref` to the recovered subset, and an explicit `unavailable_reason` per unparseable field. Partial occurrences are evidence-only: they cannot author `semantic_finding_id` and cannot close a finding; projections surface a `partial_parse` flag and treat missing fields as `unknown`.\n3. **[assume_and_proceed] Plan-local compatibility files for old readers** — the precise inventory was never enumerated. Assumption: CL2 writes **new** domain events to SQLite tables only and touches no existing plan-local JSON reader paths; all existing artifact paths remain readable by old readers until the coordinated cutover in CL5. A focused compatibility inventory is deferred and not on this plan's critical path.\n4. **[assume_and_proceed] Cancelled-CL2 artifact audit** — exact SHA-256 hashes and per-artifact assessment not yet produced. Assumption: all five cancelled artifacts are pre-amendment and adopted as-is by none; a formal audit (hashes + per-artifact compliance + explicit do-not-adopt boundary) is a **required Step 13 artifact** before any cancelled code is referenced.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- CL2 proceeds against archived CL1 schema/replay contracts (bridge; see Anchor tension). The ported `schemas.py`/`semantic_loop.py` are byte-equivalent to the archive plus *additive* v2 fields; v1 golden serialization is preserved.\n- Additive versioning means: `cl.schema.v2` joins `cl.schema.v1` in `SUPPORTED_VERSIONS`; new v2 occurrences reject `semantic_finding_id` in strict mode (it is read-only-legacy); v1 records read back via preserve mode.\n- The five cancelled CL2 artifacts are treated as **read-only reference**, never imported; Step 13 produces the audit before any pattern reuse.\n- WBC substrate contracts (`payload_policy.py`, `durable_refs.py`, `attempt_ledger_store.py`, `ledger_outbox.py`, `ledger_migrations.py`) are **reused, not modified** — CL2 feeds migration tuples to the existing `SqliteLedgerMigrator` and composes `SqliteAttemptLedgerStore` + `SqliteLedgerOutbox` rather than forking them.\n- Replay is strictly read-only: it scans event tables ordered by sequence, calls the pure `semantic_loop` functions, and writes nothing.\n\n## Main Work\n\n### Phase 1 — Port and additively version the CL1 domain schemas\n\n### Step 1: Port the CL1 module verbatim and register cl.schema.v2 (`arnold/critique_ledger/`)\n**Scope:** Small — Complexity: 2\n1. **Copy** the three frozen CL1 modules from the paused-epic archive into the current workspace at `arnold/critique_ledger/`: `schemas.py`, `semantic_loop.py`, `__init__.py`. These already import via `arnold.critique_ledger.schemas`, so the relative import path resolves once the package lands.\n2. **Add** `cl.schema.v2` to `SUPPORTED_VERSIONS` in `schemas.py` (currently `SUPPORTED_VERSIONS = {SCHEMA_VERSION}` with `SCHEMA_VERSION = \"cl.schema.v1\"`). Introduce `SCHEMA_VERSION_V2 = \"cl.schema.v2\"` and set `SUPPORTED_VERSIONS = {SCHEMA_VERSION, SCHEMA_VERSION_V2}`. No field changes yet — this step only proves the package imports and both versions validate.\n3. **Verify** byte-equivalence to the archive: `python -c \"import arnold.critique_ledger\"` succeeds; a quick golden round-trip of one v1 `CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope` serializes/deserializes identically.\n\n### Step 2: Additively version v2 occurrence + reconciliation fields (`arnold/critique_ledger/schemas.py`)\n**Scope:** Medium — Complexity: 3\n1. **Deprecate `semantic_finding_id` on new occurrences**: in `CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope.from_dict`, when `schema_version == cl.schema.v2` and mode is `strict`, raise `ValueError` if `semantic_finding_id` is present and non-empty (new v2 writes cannot author it). v1 reads and v2 `preserve` reads retain it for legacy compatibility. Keep the field on the dataclass so v1 round-trips stay byte-equivalent.\n2. **Extend `FindingReconciliationEvent`** additively: add `supersedes_event_id` (Optional[str], default None — corrections point at the prior reconciliation), `region_anchors` (tuple[str,...], default ()), `tripwire_state` (dict, default {}). Add these to the v2 known-field set and `to_dict`/`from_dict`; v1 strict reads continue to reject unknown fields, v1 preserve stores them.\n3. **Add golden tests**: v1 envelope/reconciliation serialize byte-identically to the archive contract; a v2 reconciliation with `supersedes_event_id`/`region_anchors`/`tripwire_state` round-trips; a v2 occurrence carrying `semantic_finding_id` raises in strict mode but preserves in `preserve` mode.\n\n### Step 3: Add v2 response, outcome-verification, and audit event types (`arnold/critique_ledger/schemas.py`)\n**Scope:** Medium — Complexity: 3\n1. **Define** `FindingResponseEvent` (`response_declared`): links a finding to the reviser's declared `addressed` claim plus the active-action slot it responds to. Fields: `schema_version`, `response_id`, `semantic_finding_id`, `addressed_claim` (the reviser's self-reported status), `evidence_refs`, `timestamp_utc`, `metadata`, `_extra`; strict/preserve `to_dict`/`from_dict` matching the CL1 dataclass idiom.\n2. **Define** `OutcomeVerificationEvent` (`outcome_verified`): the *independently verified* outcome, distinct from the reviser's `addressed` claim. Fields include `dealt_with|partially_dealt_with|not_dealt_with|unknown` as the verified outcome plus `evidence_refs`, `verifier_id`, `timestamp_utc`.\n3. **Define** `AuditEvent` (`audit_recorded`): independent risk-based audit of a disposition that removes a finding from the active set, plus an audit-disagreement variant. Fields: `audit_id`, `target_event_id`, `audit_outcome` (`agreed|disputed|review_required`), `risk_basis`, `evidence_refs`, `timestamp_utc`.\n4. **Add golden tests** for all three (v1 rejection of unknown fields in strict; v2 round-trip; freeze-for-hash determinism).\n\n### Phase 2 — Persistence substrate: migrations, atomic append, outbox\n\n### Step 4: Register additive domain-table and index migrations (`arnold/critique_ledger/cl2_migrations.py`)\n**Scope:** Medium — Complexity: 3\n1. **Define** `cl2_domain_migrations()` returning a tuple of `Migration` objects (versioned, checksum-anchored, all `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` / additive `ALTER TABLE`) for eight domain tables: `critique_occurrences`, `reconciliation_events`, `disposition_events`, `response_events`, `outcome_verification_events`, `audit_events`, `ledger_revision_manifests`, `region_freshness_state`. Each table stores the serialized envelope JSON plus the query columns the indexes need.\n2. **Add the six indexes** named in the brief: `(round_label, sequence)` for round replay; `reconciliation_events(semantic_finding_id)` and `disposition_events(semantic_finding_id)` for finding history; `critique_occurrences(attempt_id, occurrence_id)` for lookup; `reconciliation_events(canonical_finding_id)` for reconciliation join; `region_freshness_state(region_anchor, finding_id)` for scoped freshness. All via `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS` inside their migration's transaction.\n3. **Verify** by constructing `SqliteLedgerMigrator(store, cl2_domain_migrations())`, running `migrate()` twice (idempotent), asserting `MigrationState.is_complete` with no orphans/checksum mismatches, and confirming forward-fix rejection when a migration's statements are edited after apply.\n\n### Step 5: Build the domain append core — atomic occurrence + manifest + outbox (`arnold/critique_ledger/store.py`)\n**Scope:** Large — Complexity: 4\n1. **Define** `CritiqueLedgerStore` that wraps an opened `SqliteAttemptLedgerStore` + `SqliteLedgerOutbox`, runs the CL2 migration registry through `SqliteLedgerMigrator` at construction, and reuses the store's WAL connection (`store.conn`) so every domain write shares the same BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction space as the outbox (zero-network-cost atomicity).\n2. **Implement** `append_occurrence(...)`: mirror `_append_tx` exactly — `_begin_immediate_retry`, then idempotency-key dedup (return existing on exact duplicate, quarantine on divergent), then INSERT into `critique_occurrences`, then UPDATE the per-revision manifest row, then write the outbox record, all inside one `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`/`COMMIT` (ROLLBACK on any failure). Enforce that a partial publication can never expose an accepted incomplete revision: the manifest row is only marked complete/accepted inside the same transaction that validates full occurrence accounting.\n3. **Add** start-persistence-before-dispatch semantics: a `begin_critic_attempt` append that must precede any result append; terminal failure returns `persistence_failed|indeterminate`, never clean success.\n4. **Tests**: atomicity (occurrence INSERT + manifest UPDATE + outbox INSERT in one tx; simulated mid-transaction failure leaves no partial state), idempotency-key dedup prevents double-insert, divergent-duplicate quarantine, partial-publication cannot expose incomplete revision.\n\n### Step 6: Append reconciliation/disposition/response/outcome/audit/correction events (`arnold/critique_ledger/store.py`)\n**Scope:** Large — Complexity: 4\n1. **Implement** appenders for `FindingReconciliationEvent`, `FindingDispositionEvent`, `FindingResponseEvent`, `OutcomeVerificationEvent`, `AuditEvent`, each reusing the Step 5 transaction core (idempotency dedup + manifest update + outbox record in one BEGIN IMMEDIATE). Occurrences are never merged — appenders only add events.\n2. **Implement correction/supersession**: appending a reconciliation whose `supersedes_event_id` points at a prior one is an append (never an update); both events are retained immutably. Reconciliation decisions are immutable evidence — corrections append, they do not rewrite.\n3. **Tests**: correction appends a superseding decision and replay retains both; audit-disagreement event is appendable and visible; response `addressed` claim is stored separately from the outcome-verification event; semantic finding identity is assigned only by a reconciliation event, never derivable from a raw occurrence.\n\n### Phase 3 — Projections and read-only replay\n\n### Step 7: Cumulative/domain projection + read-only replay scanner (`arnold/critique_ledger/projections.py`)\n**Scope:** Medium — Complexity: 3\n1. **Implement** `replay(store)`: scan `critique_occurrences`, `reconciliation_events`, `disposition_events`, `response_events`, `outcome_verification_events`, `audit_events` ordered by `(round_label, sequence)`, feed the pure `semantic_loop` functions (`apply_reconciliation_events`, `apply_disposition_events`, etc.), and return the cumulative/domain projection. **Writes nothing** — issues no model call or external effect; it is a pure read over the WAL snapshot.\n2. **Implement** `construct_manifest`/`build_briefing` reuse so the cumulative projection is byte-equivalent to replaying the same event stream twice.\n3. **Tests**: deterministic replay over all stored events produces byte-equivalent cumulative projection; replaying twice is a no-op equality; replay mutates no store/outbox/metadata rows.\n\n### Step 8: Active-action projection + decision state (`arnold/critique_ledger/projections.py`)\n**Scope:** Medium — Complexity: 3\n1. **Implement** the bounded active-action projection exposing `must_act|must_revalidate|context_only` queues, derived from the cumulative ledger but **separate** from it so closed history does not grow the reviser workload. Closed/superseded findings land in `context_only`, not `must_act`.\n2. **Derive** explicit `accepted|review_required|disputed|superseded` decision state from the reconciliation/correction/audit stream; gate projections may rely only on currently-accepted decisions (superseded decisions do not support a clean gate projection).\n3. **Tests**: closed history does not grow the active set; closed/superseded findings appear in `context_only`; batch actions enumerate every finding ID; superseded decision does not produce a clean gate row.\n\n### Step 9: Per-round accountability projection (`arnold/critique_ledger/projections.py`)\n**Scope:** Large — Complexity: 4\n1. **Implement** the per-round accountability receipt mapping critic attempt → occurrence → logical finding/reconciliation → response/non-action → verified outcome → reopen condition, accounting for **every** parseable occurrence and active finding. Structured non-action is recorded per active requested finding (boilerplate/unauditable non-action cannot remove a finding from the active set).\n2. **Enforce** that the reviser's `addressed` claim (from `FindingResponseEvent`) remains distinct from the independently verified `dealt_with|partially_dealt_with|not_dealt_with|unknown` outcome (from `OutcomeVerificationEvent`).\n3. **Tests**: a round receipt accounts for every occurrence and active finding; `addressed` ≠ verified outcome when they disagree; a missing response leaves the finding open with an explicit `unknown` rather than inferring closure.\n\n### Phase 4 — Import, freshness, and fault\n\n### Step 10: One-time legacy importer (`arnold/critique_ledger/legacy_import.py`)\n**Scope:** Large — Complexity: 4\n1. **Implement** the importer over retained legacy M6 artifacts (v1–v5 rounds): map to v1 schemas via preserve mode, classify any legacy `semantic_finding_id` as `legacy` (read-compat only), mark semantic gaps `legacy_unknown`, and **reject unsupported target versions without partial publish**.\n2. **Handle partially-parseable attempts** per the assumption: admit with `ParseStatus.PARTIAL_RECOVERED`, an `evidence_ref` to the recovered subset, and `unavailable_reason` per unparseable field; never promote the invalid portion or synthesize fields. Partial occurrences are evidence-only.\n3. **Never invent** duplicate/resolution/evidence/reopen relationships during import (historical gaps stay explicit).\n4. **Fixture tests**: current-source, target-only, future-version (rejected), corrupt (PARTIAL_RECOVERED), redacted (surfaced as unknown, not leaked), unavailable, duplicate (idempotent), out-of-order, concurrent, and crash fixtures all handled correctly.\n\n### Step 11: Region freshness vectors + deterministic tripwires + governed tombstone (`arnold/critique_ledger/freshness.py`)\n**Scope:** Large — Complexity: 4\n1. **Implement** region-anchor freshness: each finding declares stable section identity + content hashes in `region_freshness_state`. A region change marks only affected scoped findings stale without forcing unrelated findings through full semantic re-adjudication. Unknown or cross-cutting scope defaults to whole-plan freshness; region movement/split/merge becomes an unresolved mapping (not a silent re-map).\n2. **Implement deterministic tripwires** that fire `needs_reconciliation` (never semantic merge/closure/suppression) when a plan region changes, evidence/dependency changes, a near-match occurs, an audit disagrees, or a model/schema revision is stale.\n3. **Implement governed tombstone/unavailable handling**: surface missing/redacted evidence as `unknown` in projections without crashing replay; tombstone state is visible and never silently suppresses a finding.\n4. **Tests**: region-anchor change stales only affected findings; unchanged regions stay fresh; tripwire fires `needs_reconciliation` only; tombstoned/unavailable evidence surfaces as `unknown`; whole-plan default for unknown scope.\n\n### Step 12: Fault, concurrency, negative-mutations, privacy, compatibility tests\n**Scope:** Medium — Complexity: 3\n1. **Concurrency/crash**: concurrent writers respect WAL single-writer (busy-retry); crash mid-append leaves the DB consistent; outbox `get_stale_pending`/`retry_failed` recover; idempotency dedup survives crash-restart; duplicate replay is a no-op.\n2. **Negative-mutation**: prove ledger/replay cannot mutate plan, gate, lifecycle, queue, Git/provider, delivery, or external-effect state; projections carry no positive authority; replay is strictly read-only.\n3. **Privacy/compatibility**: redacted evidence is surfaced as unknown (never leaked); existing critique-custody suites pass; WBC ledger/evidence suites pass; plan-local compatibility files remain readable by old readers.\n4. **WBC reuse assertion**: CL2 imports inline/reference, retention, redaction, ordering, and effect rules from `payload_policy.py`/`durable_refs.py` rather than reimplementing them.\n\n### Phase 5 — Audit and handoff\n\n### Step 13: Audit the cancelled CL2 archive and write the do-not-adopt boundary\n**Scope:** Small — Complexity: 2\n1. **Inventory** the five cancelled artifacts (`cl2_migrations.py`, `m6_importer.py`, `ledger_writer.py`, `wbc_bridge.py`, `manifest_store.py`) with **SHA-256 hashes** computed from the frozen archive bytes.\n2. **Assess** each artifact against the amended contract: none supports corrections/supersession, independent audit events, plan-region/dependency anchors, tripwire state, response/outcome events, active-action projection, or per-round accountability projection. Record the explicit do-not-adopt boundary and any reusable *structural* pattern (BEGIN IMMEDIATE wiring, store-adapter shape) that still satisfies the amended contract.\n3. **Write** the audit to `docs/critique-ledger/cl2/cancelled-cl2-audit.md`. No cancelled code is referenced before this artifact exists.\n\n### Step 14: Write and review the CL3 handoff (`docs/critique-ledger/handoffs/cl2-ledger-replay.json`)\n**Scope:** Small — Complexity: 2\n1. **Write** `docs/critique-ledger/handoffs/cl2-ledger-replay.json` containing: API/schema hashes; backend/atomicity decision (reuse `SqliteAttemptLedgerStore`+`SqliteLedgerOutbox`, single BEGIN IMMEDIATE tx); the one-time import map; replay hashes for all three projections; the fixture index; region/freshness and tripwire rules; audit/correction event rules; response/outcome event rules; active-projection bounds; round-receipt hashes; fault/concurrency results; backup/restore prerequisites; and unresolved limits (including the open CL1-handoff-acceptance blocker).\n2. **Note** the recorded anchor tension (CL1 handoff `accepted_for_cl2: false`) as an unresolved limit so CL3 validates the handoff and replay fixtures before constructing a briefing.\n\n## Execution Order\n1. **Phase 1 (Steps 1–3)** first: the schemas must compile and golden before any persistence can store or read them. Port verbatim (Step 1) before additive versioning (Steps 2–3) so v1 byte-equivalence is provable in isolation.\n2. **Phase 2 (Steps 4–6)** next: migrations (Step 4) before the append core (Step 5) before the typed appenders (Step 6) — each layer depends on the one before, and the append core establishes the transaction pattern all appenders reuse.\n3. **Phase 3 (Steps 7–9)** after persistence: replay (Step 7) before the two higher projections (Steps 8–9) because both are rebuildable views over replay.\n4. **Phase 4 (Steps 10–12)** can proceed once replay is stable: importer (10), freshness/tripwires (11), then the cross-cutting fault/privacy/compat suite (12) which validates everything together.\n5. **Phase 5 (Steps 13–14)** last: the cancelled-archive audit (13) is a gate before referencing any archive pattern, and the handoff (14) captures hashes from the now-frozen implementation.\n\n## Validation Order\n1. **Cheapest first, per phase**: import + golden round-trip (Step 1) before versioning logic; migration idempotency/checksum (Step 4) before append atomicity (Step 5).\n2. **Property tests alongside each step**: every Step 2–12 carries its own focused tests (golden, atomicity, determinism, bounds, freshness) written in the same turn so the worker can self-verify before handing off.\n3. **Broad suite last (Step 12)**: run the existing critique-custody and WBC ledger/evidence suites plus the new concurrency/crash/negative/privacy tests only after the replay + projections are wired, to prove no regression and no positive-authority leakage.\n4. **Final hash capture (Step 14)**: replay/projection/handoff hashes are recorded after the full suite is green so the handoff is reproducible by CL3.\n\n## Complexity summary\n- Step 1: 2 (verbatim port + one constant).\n- Step 2: 3 (additive occurrence/reconciliation versioning + strict-mode logic).\n- Step 3: 3 (three new event dataclasses + golden).\n- Step 4: 3 (eight tables + six indexes in the migration framework).\n- Step 5: 4 (transaction-core append mirroring `_append_tx` + atomicity tests).\n- Step 6: 4 (typed appenders + correction/supersession retention).\n- Step 7: 3 (read-only replay + cumulative projection + determinism).\n- Step 8: 3 (bounded active-action projection + decision state).\n- Step 9: 4 (per-round accountability receipt linking five stages).\n- Step 10: 4 (one-time importer + ten fixture categories).\n- Step 11: 4 (region freshness + tripwires + governed tombstone).\n- Step 12: 3 (concurrency/crash/negative/privacy/compat suite).\n- Step 13: 2 (cancelled-archive audit + do-not-adopt boundary).\n- Step 14: 2 (CL3 handoff JSON).\n\n## Success criteria\n\n```json\n[\n  {\"criterion\": \"Ported CL1 v1 schemas (CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope, FindingReconciliationEvent, FindingDispositionEvent, DomainBriefingEnvelope, LedgerRevisionManifest) serialize/deserialize byte-equivalently to the frozen archive contract; golden round-trip tests pass\", \"priority\": \"must\", \"requires\": [\"run_tests\"]},\n  {\"criterion\": \"v2 additive schema is correct: new v2 occurrences reject semantic_finding_id in strict mode; FindingReconciliationEvent carries supersedes_event_id/region_anchors/tripwire_state; FindingResponseEvent, OutcomeVerificationEvent, AuditEvent round-trip; v1 records still read via preserve mode\", \"priority\": \"must\", \"requires\": [\"run_tests\"]},\n  {\"criterion\": \"Eight domain tables and six indexes register as additive, checksum-validated, idempotent migrations in SqliteLedgerMigrator; migrate() is idempotent and forward-fix violations are rejected\", \"priority\": \"must\", \"requires\": [\"run_tests\"]},\n  {\"criterion\": \"Occurrence INSERT + revision-manifest UPDATE + outbox INSERT execute in one BEGIN IMMEDIATE; simulated mid-transaction failure leaves no partial state; idempotency-key dedup prevents double-insert; partial publication cannot expose an accepted incomplete revision\", \"priority\": \"must\", \"requires\": [\"run_tests\"]},\n  {\"criterion\": \"Correction appends a superseding reconciliation; replay retains both original and superseding events and derives latest accepted|review_required|disputed|superseded decision state; superseded decisions do not support a clean gate projection\", \"priority\": \"must\", \"requires\": [\"run_tests\"]},\n  {\"criterion\": \"Deterministic read-only replay over all stored events produces byte-equivalent cumulative projection; replaying twice is a no-op equality; replay issues no model call, external effect, or store mutation\", \"priority\": \"must\", \"requires\": [\"run_tests\"]},\n  {\"criterion\": \"Active-action projection exposes must_act|must_revalidate|context_only queues; closed history does not grow the active set; closed/superseded findings appear in context_only not must_act; batch actions enumerate every finding ID\", \"priority\": \"must\", \"requires\": [\"run_tests\"]},\n  {\"criterion\": \"Per-round accountability receipt maps attempt->occurrence->finding->response/non-action->verified outcome->reopen condition and accounts for every parseable occurrence and active finding; reviser 'addressed' claim is distinct from independently verified dealt_with|partially_dealt_with|not_dealt_with|unknown\", \"priority\": \"must\", \"requires\": [\"run_tests\"]},\n  {\"criterion\": \"Region-anchor change stales only affected scoped findings; unchanged regions do not re-reconcile; unknown/cross-cutting scope defaults to whole-plan freshness; region movement